1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,700
ISA, ISK, PEA, Depot.

2
00:00:01,720 --> 00:00:04,020
Every country in Europe seems

3
00:00:04,040 --> 00:00:06,730
to have its own investment accounts,

4
00:00:06,750 --> 00:00:08,270
with their own strange letters.

5
00:00:09,570 --> 00:00:10,990
But almost all of them fit

6
00:00:11,010 --> 00:00:12,180
into one of ten boxes.

7
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:14,420
Some save you tax.

8
00:00:14,440 --> 00:00:16,450
Some lock your money for decades.

9
00:00:16,470 --> 00:00:17,480
And some are traps.

10
00:00:18,770 --> 00:00:20,570
The account you pick can matter as

11
00:00:20,590 --> 00:00:22,210
much as what you put inside it.

12
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:26,420
Rules change every year and differ by country.

13
00:00:26,440 --> 00:00:29,920
This is education, not tax or financial advice.

14
00:00:31,230 --> 00:00:32,900
Let's open the first box.

15
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:34,730
Box 1.

16
00:00:34,750 --> 00:00:36,110
The savings account.

17
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:38,570
The simplest one.

18
00:00:38,590 --> 00:00:39,850
Your money sits at the bank,

19
00:00:39,870 --> 00:00:41,420
earns a little interest,

20
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:43,010
and you can take it out any time.

21
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:44,940
In the EU,

22
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:48,510
deposits are protected up to €100,000 per person,

23
00:00:48,530 --> 00:00:50,520
per bank, if the bank fails.

24
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:54,480
Some countries add tax-free savings accounts,

25
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:55,990
like the Livret A in France,

26
00:00:56,010 --> 00:00:57,070
but with a ceiling.

27
00:00:58,370 --> 00:00:59,970
The catch is inflation.

28
00:00:59,990 --> 00:01:02,640
When prices rise faster than your interest,

29
00:01:02,660 --> 00:01:04,450
your money quietly loses value.

30
00:01:05,770 --> 00:01:06,740
A savings account

31
00:01:06,760 --> 00:01:08,270
is for money you need soon,

32
00:01:08,290 --> 00:01:09,900
not for the next thirty years.

33
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:11,690
Box 2.

34
00:01:11,710 --> 00:01:13,120
The brokerage account.

35
00:01:14,430 --> 00:01:16,560
A brokerage account lets you buy shares,

36
00:01:16,580 --> 00:01:18,330
ETFs and bonds.

37
00:01:18,350 --> 00:01:19,990
No limits, no lock-up,

38
00:01:20,010 --> 00:01:22,020
but usually no tax advantage either.

39
00:01:23,330 --> 00:01:25,670
In Germany, the first €1,000

40
00:01:25,690 --> 00:01:28,250
of investment income each year is tax-free.

41
00:01:28,270 --> 00:01:31,100
After that, you pay about 26%.

42
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:34,490
In France, gains on a regular account

43
00:01:34,510 --> 00:01:38,340
are taxed at 31.4% since 2026.

44
00:01:39,630 --> 00:01:42,980
And in Ireland, ETFs are taxed at 38%,

45
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:44,100
and every eight years,

46
00:01:44,120 --> 00:01:45,420
even if you never sell.

47
00:01:46,730 --> 00:01:49,190
Most countries tax your gains when you sell.

48
00:01:49,210 --> 00:01:50,990
Others, like the Netherlands,

49
00:01:51,010 --> 00:01:52,150
tax an assumed return

50
00:01:52,170 --> 00:01:53,730
on your savings every year.

51
00:01:55,030 --> 00:01:56,810
It's the most flexible box.

52
00:01:56,830 --> 00:01:59,240
But flexibility usually costs you tax.

53
00:02:00,530 --> 00:02:01,100
Box 3.

54
00:02:01,120 --> 00:02:02,990
The tax-free investment account.

55
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:06,300
This is the box many people miss:

56
00:02:06,320 --> 00:02:07,810
an investment account with

57
00:02:07,830 --> 00:02:09,070
a tax break built in.

58
00:02:10,370 --> 00:02:12,030
Sweden has the ISK.

59
00:02:12,050 --> 00:02:13,760
Britain has the ISA.

60
00:02:13,780 --> 00:02:15,430
France has the PEA.

61
00:02:15,450 --> 00:02:18,080
Denmark, Finland and Italy have their own versions.

62
00:02:19,370 --> 00:02:20,580
In Sweden's ISK,

63
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:22,470
you don't pay tax on your gains.

64
00:02:22,490 --> 00:02:23,600
You pay a small flat

65
00:02:23,620 --> 00:02:25,210
tax on the account's value,

66
00:02:25,230 --> 00:02:26,980
about 1% a year.

67
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:31,350
Since 2026, the first 300,000 kronor are tax-free.

68
00:02:32,670 --> 00:02:34,100
In Britain, you can put up

69
00:02:34,120 --> 00:02:36,730
to £20,000 a year into an ISA,

70
00:02:36,750 --> 00:02:38,800
and never pay tax on what it earns.

71
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:42,290
Denmark's share savings account taxes

72
00:02:42,310 --> 00:02:44,630
gains at 17% every year.

73
00:02:44,650 --> 00:02:47,010
That's still less than on a normal account.

74
00:02:48,300 --> 00:02:50,860
In France, after five years in a PEA,

75
00:02:50,880 --> 00:02:52,630
your gains skip income tax.

76
00:02:52,650 --> 00:02:54,600
You only pay social charges.

77
00:02:55,900 --> 00:02:57,900
In 2025, the European

78
00:02:57,920 --> 00:02:59,970
Commission asked every member state

79
00:02:59,990 --> 00:03:01,720
to create accounts like these.

80
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:04,470
So if your country has one,

81
00:03:04,490 --> 00:03:05,480
it's worth a look.

82
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:07,790
Just check the rules and the limits first.

83
00:03:09,100 --> 00:03:09,750
Box 4.

84
00:03:09,770 --> 00:03:10,890
Life insurance.

85
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:13,270
In some countries,

86
00:03:13,290 --> 00:03:15,750
life insurance isn't really about dying.

87
00:03:15,770 --> 00:03:17,370
It's an investment wrapper.

88
00:03:18,670 --> 00:03:20,580
In France, people hold about two

89
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:22,970
trillion euros in the assurance-vie.

90
00:03:22,990 --> 00:03:24,500
After eight years,

91
00:03:24,520 --> 00:03:26,130
up to €4,600

92
00:03:26,150 --> 00:03:28,060
of gains you withdraw each year

93
00:03:28,080 --> 00:03:29,470
are free of income tax.

94
00:03:30,770 --> 00:03:32,810
It's also used for inheritance.

95
00:03:32,830 --> 00:03:35,180
In France, each beneficiary can receive up

96
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:41,040
to €152,500 without inheritance tax, under conditions.

97
00:03:42,330 --> 00:03:45,430
Belgium, Italy and Luxembourg have similar contracts,

98
00:03:45,450 --> 00:03:48,200
with funds and sometimes ETFs inside.

99
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:50,730
The catch is fees.

100
00:03:50,750 --> 00:03:52,500
The insurer takes a yearly fee,

101
00:03:52,520 --> 00:03:54,420
on top of the funds' own costs.

102
00:03:55,730 --> 00:03:57,590
A cheap contract can be great.

103
00:03:57,610 --> 00:04:00,270
An expensive one quietly eats your returns.

104
00:04:01,570 --> 00:04:02,140
Box 5.

105
00:04:02,160 --> 00:04:03,530
The personal pension.

106
00:04:04,830 --> 00:04:06,620
Personal pension accounts give

107
00:04:06,640 --> 00:04:07,840
you a tax break today,

108
00:04:07,860 --> 00:04:10,950
in exchange for locking your money until retirement.

109
00:04:12,270 --> 00:04:13,890
France has the PER.

110
00:04:13,910 --> 00:04:16,500
Germany has Riester and Rürup plans.

111
00:04:16,520 --> 00:04:18,300
Spain has pension plans,

112
00:04:18,320 --> 00:04:20,990
and Poland has the IKE and IKZE.

113
00:04:22,300 --> 00:04:23,990
Each country sets a limit.

114
00:04:24,010 --> 00:04:26,280
In Spain, it's just €1,500

115
00:04:26,300 --> 00:04:27,910
a year for a personal plan.

116
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:31,910
The EU even created a pan-European pension,

117
00:04:31,930 --> 00:04:33,860
the PEPP, in 2022.

118
00:04:33,880 --> 00:04:36,870
Four years later, only two providers offer it.

119
00:04:38,170 --> 00:04:39,750
The tax break is real.

120
00:04:39,770 --> 00:04:40,960
But these accounts are built

121
00:04:40,980 --> 00:04:43,020
for money you won't need for decades.

122
00:04:44,330 --> 00:04:44,850
Box 6.

123
00:04:44,870 --> 00:04:46,150
The workplace plan.

124
00:04:47,470 --> 00:04:50,320
Many employers offer a pension or savings plan,

125
00:04:50,340 --> 00:04:52,340
and some add money on top of yours.

126
00:04:53,630 --> 00:04:55,150
In Germany, if you put part

127
00:04:55,170 --> 00:04:57,320
of your salary into a company pension,

128
00:04:57,340 --> 00:05:00,110
your employer usually has to add 15%.

129
00:05:01,400 --> 00:05:03,230
In France, many companies top up

130
00:05:03,250 --> 00:05:05,690
what you put into the company savings plan.

131
00:05:05,710 --> 00:05:07,950
That top-up is called the abondement.

132
00:05:09,230 --> 00:05:10,960
Free money from your employer

133
00:05:10,980 --> 00:05:13,220
is the closest thing to a guaranteed return.

134
00:05:13,240 --> 00:05:14,650
Read your contract.

135
00:05:15,930 --> 00:05:16,510
Box 7.

136
00:05:16,530 --> 00:05:17,830
Accounts for children.

137
00:05:19,130 --> 00:05:22,050
Some countries have investment accounts for children.

138
00:05:22,070 --> 00:05:24,590
In Britain, the Junior ISA takes up

139
00:05:24,610 --> 00:05:26,460
to £9,000 a year.

140
00:05:27,770 --> 00:05:29,560
Germany plans to put €10

141
00:05:29,580 --> 00:05:31,600
a month into an investment account

142
00:05:31,620 --> 00:05:33,500
for every child from 6 to 18,

143
00:05:33,520 --> 00:05:37,170
starting in 2027, if parliament approves it.

144
00:05:38,470 --> 00:05:39,880
Money invested early has

145
00:05:39,900 --> 00:05:41,400
the longest time to grow.

146
00:05:41,420 --> 00:05:42,470
And time is the one

147
00:05:42,490 --> 00:05:43,980
thing kids have plenty of.

148
00:05:45,270 --> 00:05:45,950
Box 8.

149
00:05:45,970 --> 00:05:47,160
The robo-advisor.

150
00:05:48,470 --> 00:05:50,590
A robo-advisor invests for you.

151
00:05:50,610 --> 00:05:52,340
You answer a few questions,

152
00:05:52,360 --> 00:05:54,030
and it picks a mix of ETFs

153
00:05:54,050 --> 00:05:55,190
and rebalances it.

154
00:05:56,500 --> 00:05:57,690
It's convenient.

155
00:05:57,710 --> 00:06:00,290
But the service often costs between a quarter

156
00:06:00,310 --> 00:06:02,230
and three quarters of a percent a year,

157
00:06:02,250 --> 00:06:04,620
on top of the ETFs' own fees.

158
00:06:05,930 --> 00:06:07,920
Over decades, that fee adds up.

159
00:06:07,940 --> 00:06:10,020
You're paying for not having to decide.

160
00:06:11,330 --> 00:06:14,600
Some robo-advisors work inside a tax-free account

161
00:06:14,620 --> 00:06:16,240
or a life insurance contract.

162
00:06:16,260 --> 00:06:18,030
So two boxes can stack.

163
00:06:19,330 --> 00:06:19,900
Box 9.

164
00:06:19,920 --> 00:06:21,220
The trading account.

165
00:06:22,500 --> 00:06:25,110
This box looks like investing, but it isn't.

166
00:06:25,130 --> 00:06:27,540
CFD and leveraged trading accounts let

167
00:06:27,560 --> 00:06:29,840
you bet on prices with borrowed money.

168
00:06:31,130 --> 00:06:33,460
When EU regulators looked at the data,

169
00:06:33,480 --> 00:06:34,460
between 74

170
00:06:34,480 --> 00:06:37,500
and 89% of retail accounts lost money.

171
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:40,610
That's why these brokers must show

172
00:06:40,630 --> 00:06:42,210
that percentage in their ads.

173
00:06:42,230 --> 00:06:42,460
If you see it,

174
00:06:42,480 --> 00:06:44,400
you know which box you're in.

175
00:06:45,700 --> 00:06:47,610
Some apps make it feel like a game.

176
00:06:47,630 --> 00:06:50,160
Notifications, bright colours, quick trades.

177
00:06:50,180 --> 00:06:51,520
That's not an accident.

178
00:06:52,800 --> 00:06:53,310
Box 10.

179
00:06:53,330 --> 00:06:54,580
The foreign broker.

180
00:06:55,870 --> 00:06:57,840
Many Europeans open an account with

181
00:06:57,860 --> 00:06:59,440
a broker in another country,

182
00:06:59,460 --> 00:07:00,580
because it's cheaper.

183
00:07:01,870 --> 00:07:03,990
But a foreign broker usually doesn't

184
00:07:04,010 --> 00:07:05,750
handle your home country's taxes.

185
00:07:05,770 --> 00:07:07,730
So the paperwork becomes yours.

186
00:07:09,030 --> 00:07:11,730
In France, forgetting to declare a foreign account

187
00:07:11,750 --> 00:07:14,580
can cost €1,500 per account, per year.

188
00:07:15,870 --> 00:07:17,710
In Germany, you declare the gains

189
00:07:17,730 --> 00:07:19,130
yourself in your tax return.

190
00:07:19,150 --> 00:07:20,850
Cheaper fees, more homework.

191
00:07:22,130 --> 00:07:24,010
And Spain asks for a special form if

192
00:07:24,030 --> 00:07:27,090
your assets abroad are worth more than €50,000.

193
00:07:28,400 --> 00:07:30,040
Those are the ten boxes.

194
00:07:30,060 --> 00:07:31,300
Most people only use two

195
00:07:31,320 --> 00:07:32,330
or three of them.

196
00:07:33,630 --> 00:07:34,700
A common order:

197
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:35,840
a savings cushion first,

198
00:07:35,860 --> 00:07:38,340
then any free money from your employer,

199
00:07:38,360 --> 00:07:39,430
then a tax-free account

200
00:07:39,450 --> 00:07:40,820
if your country has one.

201
00:07:42,100 --> 00:07:43,600
Rules change every year,

202
00:07:43,620 --> 00:07:45,280
and every country is different.

203
00:07:45,300 --> 00:07:47,560
Check your tax office's website.

204
00:07:47,580 --> 00:07:49,450
This is not financial advice.

205
00:07:50,730 --> 00:07:51,670
Ten boxes.

206
00:07:51,690 --> 00:07:54,460
Now, when someone says ISA, PEA or Depot,

207
00:07:54,480 --> 00:07:56,140
you'll know which box it belongs in.

208
00:07:57,430 --> 00:07:59,000
If this helped, subscribe.

209
00:07:59,020 --> 00:08:01,760
Next up, every type of ETF, explained.

